End-to-end tests: a browser driving a real cca.

Run them with `make check-e2e` from the repository root, which builds
the binary first. They need PostgreSQL (the same setup the
internal/db tests want) and a browser in PATH: chromium, chromium-browser or
google-chrome, or one named by CCA_E2E_CHROMIUM. A browser is never
downloaded, and a missing one is an error rather than a skip — a suite
that quietly does not run is worse than one that fails.

What is real and what is not
----------------------------

Real: the cca binary, the production schema from internal/db/schemas,
the JSON API, the websocket, and the frontends. There is no stub
backend. A stub is a second implementation of the API, and it drifts
from the first silently — the drift is only noticed when it hides a
bug rather than when it is introduced.

Not real: the identity provider. The tests serve an empty JWKS so the
server gets past startup, and mint the cookie the OIDC callback would
have set by shelling out to `cca -session`. Nothing is seeded into the
database for this, because there is nothing to seed: a session is a
signed cookie and no table records one. Nothing under test lives on
that path.

Why these tests and not more
----------------------------

Anything one process can answer belongs in a cheaper test:
internal/db/*_test.go for the schema rules and for what they do under
concurrent sessions, internal/web/*_test.go
for the handlers and the hub, svelte-check and eslint for the frontend.
What is left over, and what lives here, is the joins between them: that
the JSON the handlers emit is the shape the components read, that a rule
the database enforces is also shown to the user before they hit it, and
that an event travels from one browser through the hub to another.

The frontends are embedded in the binary
----------------------------------------

ui/embed.go embeds student/dist and admin/dist at compile time, so a
frontend change is not visible to these tests until the *binary* is
rebuilt. `make check-e2e` depends on the binary for that reason; running
`npm run check:e2e` by hand after editing a component will silently test
the previously embedded bundle.

Layout
------

backend         POSIX sh: scratch database, schema, seed, run ./cca
seed.sql        the fixture: vocabulary, courses, students. No
                sessions; harness.ts mints those cookies with
                `cca -session`
harness.ts      per-file stack: jwks stub, backend, browser, teardown
*.test.ts       node:test specs

They compile to node_modules/.e2e with the repository's own typescript
and run under node --test. playwright-core is used rather than
playwright because it never downloads a browser.
